Separate stored energy from heat and work along a process (@physics-energy-paths: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/balances/Closed-system-energy-and-storage.lingua#r_26CN1Z2H23NCXB8W7K74CVW5VN") { r_0F096B911F1CDDD09E34008C73
Use Q>0 for heat entering the chosen closed system and W_by>0 for work it does on the surroundings. The first law is ΔE=Q−W_by, where E includes internal energy U and any relevant macroscopic kinetic and potential energy. When those macroscopic changes are negligible, ΔU=Q−W_by. In the chemistry convention with work on the system positive, W_on=−W_by and ΔU=Q+W_on. The numerical physical account is unchanged. Reuse [Define a thermal boundary before relating temperature heat and enthalpy](../../chemistry/core/Define-a-thermal-boundary-before-relating-temperature-heat-and-enthalpy.lingua#r_0944AD7B55F0D1168D9ACB8E2A), [Choose a system and track heat, work, and enthalpy](../../chemistry/general-chemistry/energy/Systems-heat-work-and-enthalpy.lingua#r_545JZM2734935DFWXBV90WM5PE) and [Heat and work signs across physics and chemistry](../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/foundations/Heat-and-work-signs-across-physics-and-chemistry.lingua#r_3Z4G73ZME2A7M1A63NGX177SQN) instead of treating their conventions as conflicting laws.

Internal energy is a state property for a specified material state description. Heat and work describe transfers over a process; they are not two separately stored substances inside a body. An electrical supply crossing the boundary of a rigid insulated system is work input, even if it later raises the temperature. With Q=0 and 30 J electrical input, W_by=−30 J and ΔU=30 J. A rigid boundary excludes its volume-displacement work, not electrical or shaft work. If the boundary is redrawn around a different subsystem, the identified transfer mechanisms must be redrawn with it.

For the later idealized gas study, fix nR=1 J/K and use the monatomic caloric model U=(3/2)nRT+constant. The total constant-volume heat capacity is C_V,total=3nR/2=1.5 J/K. Only when the system is closed, volume is fixed, other work is absent and macroscopic energy changes are negligible does heat alone satisfy Q=C_V,total ΔT in this model. The 30 J electrical example would also raise this model’s temperature by 20 K, but its energy input is work rather than heat. Identical final temperatures do not identify the transfer history.

A general temperature-dependent capacity gives a state-energy change by integrating the appropriate derivative under its specified constraints. The new [Connect accumulated change and local rate under stated conditions](../../mathematics/core/Connect-accumulated-change-and-local-rate-under-stated-conditions.lingua#r_0005E569C642147DA7E568DFE0) supplies rate/accumulation language; it does not determine the material function. The common block uses a constant capacity to keep that extra assumption visible. The shared chemical calorimetry and phase study already show why energy can enter phase change instead of a simple temperature increase.

For two paths connecting identical initial/final states of the same closed material, ΔU is the same. Q and W_by can differ while their difference remains fixed. Over a complete cycle returning to the same state, ΔU=0 and Q_net=W_net under the same neglected macroscopic changes. This conservation relation alone neither establishes which direction is spontaneous nor allows complete conversion of heat from one reservoir into work. Heat-transfer direction, irreversibility and second-law restrictions require their own account; transport rates require still more physical information.

**Read and account:** [Introduction to Engineering Thermodynamics — Claire Yu Yan](../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/sources/Introduction-to-Engineering-Thermodynamics-Yan.lingua#r_6AX93SRMGE0XXJTQ1NW6X9QXTW), sections 4.1–4.4, and [University Physics Volume 2 — OpenStax](../electromagnetism/sources/University-Physics-Volume-2.lingua#r_5JQ0CPQ0RNJNVH175Y9V305J5Q), section 3.3. Begin with the inspected first-law and work passages; the wider chapter assignments remain attached to their later preparation. Draw a boundary for a heated gas, a gas plus heater, and the larger insulated enclosure. Label Q, each work mechanism and stored-energy terms. Explain what observation would distinguish the model’s predicted energy change from an actual measured one.

**Continue:** [Closed-system energy and storage](../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/balances/Closed-system-energy-and-storage.lingua#r_26CN1Z2H23NCXB8W7K74CVW5VN).

